SOAPBOX: I was able to transmit over a wider frequency range than I had
SOAPBOX: anticipated.
SOAPBOX: The antenna is a 30 feet tall tower and 10 feet mast with a #10 AWG insulated shunt wire connected to the tower at 20 feet up from the bottom by an aluminum angle 1 inch by 1 inch, long enough to be bolted to the tower and have the shunt wire 15 inches away from the tower. The bottom of the tower is grounded and that is the common point for the variable shunt capacitor and the coaxial cable shield. Near the bottom, the shunt wire goes through a hole in a 2 inch plastic pipe that keeps the bottom of the shunt wire 15 inches away from the tower, provides tension on the shunt wire, and provides a point to connect the shunt capacitor and the series capacitor which are both manually adjusted. The series capacitor is connected to the shunt wire and the other end of the series capacitor goes to the coaxial cable connector center. Using an MFJ259B, the two capacitors were adjusted to provide a 1:1 SWR from 1895 kHz to 1900 kHz and a 3:1 SWR from 1880 kHz to 1912 kHz. Operation outside that frequency range was achieved by using the automatic tuner in the transceiver, an ICOM IC-7600, but at lower power.
